---
/*
  /lab/notes — a LOCAL-ONLY bench, dev-gated below, linked from nowhere.

  THE QUESTION, Michael 2026-08-26: *"I noticed when I was working through my
  notes that sometimes I wish I had a full editor. If I have to make a very
  large document or just dump a bunch of information, it's not really super nice
  to have to scroll the entire page. You lose context of everything else. If I
  just dump a bunch of stuff, it becomes a huge, stretched-out editor, and I
  can't browse anything else."*

  ⚠ THE ASYMMETRY THIS BENCH IS BUILT AROUND, and it is a fact rather than a
  preference. The pile is bounded when READING and unbounded when EDITING:

    · `.dump__text--clamped` (hq.css) caps a long card at 16rem with a mask fade
      and offers `more`.
    · `.dump__shell .jot-prose` (hq.css) sets a `min-height` of 3.25rem and NO
      maximum, so the one shared editor grows to the full height of whatever
      document it is handed.

  So a 900-word dump sits as a tidy card until you press ✎, and then the page
  IS that note. That is the stretch, and it is why `capped` below is on the
  bench as a correctness fix rather than as a proposition.

  ⚠ THE TRAP: THE DEAD DOOR, REBUILT. `Add ▾ → Note` used to open a
  near-fullscreen sheet and the pile was built to kill it — *"the exact cost §2
  was written to remove"* (plan 14's LOG entry). Read again, the objection was
  the TITLE FIELD, not the fullscreen: *"I have to title these things… I see
  untitled, untitled, untitled."* So `focus` and `split` below ask for nothing
  — no title, no slug, no tabs, no publish — and if either wins, that is the
  line that must hold when it ships.

  ⚠ AND THE RIVAL THAT IS NOT ON THE BENCH IS ALREADY IN THE ROOM. → **Make a
  piece** is one status flip and puts the thought in the real composer. If a
  long dump is a piece in waiting, the answer is that this bench is unnecessary
  — which is a legitimate outcome and the first thing to check while looking.

  ── WHAT IS REAL HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT ──────────────────────────────────────

  14 §11a's rule: prototype the thing in question, not just the pixels.

    REAL · the editor. `mountRichEditor` from `src/scripts/rich-editor.ts`, with
           the pile's own options — `docClass: 'jot-prose'`, `breaks: true`, the
           shipped `EditorToolbar`. Typing, the toolbar and paste behave here as
           they do there.
    REAL · ONE editor for the whole pile, moved. The shipped room's constraint
           (notes.astro: *"a room that lists a hundred jottings cannot mount a
           hundred of those"*) is what makes `focus` cheap, so the bench honours
           it instead of mounting four. ProseMirror survives the move — same
           node either side of it, the /lab/capture finding.
    REAL · the CSS. `.dump`, `.dump__text`, the 16rem clamp and the shell's
           metrics are hq.css's, not retyped. `.drawer-dialog` is admin.css's,
           so the drawer already answers the phone keyboard (`--kb`) for free.
    REAL · the numbers. Page height, visible editor, reachable notes and taps
           MEASURED live, not claimed. Same argument as plan 38's ratios: a
           quoted number goes stale, a measured one cannot.
    NOT  · the save. `fragments.saveWriting`, the 700ms debounce and the
           concurrency token are IDENTICAL under all four propositions — none of
           them touches the write path, which is the argument for building any
           of this. Writing rows here would only breed junk in the real pile.
    NOT  · images. `EditorToolbar` is mounted without `image`, so no upload path
           and no Supabase browser client. Settled and unaffected.
    NOT  · the filing. Every destination — on the card, in the chooser, in the
           drawer's foot — PRINTS the row it would write and does not write it.
           All five already exist in the room and none of them is in question;
           what is judged is where they LIVE once there is a drawer to hold them.
    NOT  · the glyph. `⤢` is a character, because an icon cannot render without
           an `astro.config` allowlist line and the MARK is not what this bench
           decides. `/lab/chrome` added one line for `rss-simple` because there
           the mark WAS the question; here it is not.

  ⚠ THE SECOND QUESTION, ADDED AFTER THE FIRST LOOK (2026-08-26). Split won on
  sight — *"I like the split definitely the most"* — and immediately exposed the
  half that was not built: *"I feel like we're wasting some opportunity in that
  view to include more actions like make this a quote, append to writing,
  delete… it's missing a lot of obvious ui ux."* True, and the fix is not simply
  to transplant the card's foot, because that foot's shape was ARGUED FOR at
  card scale (14 · Piece 2: three controls, not six). The bar's second toggle
  builds both spellings — behind → and standing in the open — so the transplant
  has to win rather than arrive by default.

  ⚠ THE PHONE, WHICH IS THE THIRD QUESTION AND THE ONE WITH A HARD ANSWER
  (2026-08-26). Michael: *"how will this look on mobile, because it's hard to
  have both the editor and the sidebar to browse."* At 390px it is not hard, it
  is impossible — a 16rem rail beside a readable measure wants ~640px. So below
  768px the two panes stop being side by side and the rail slides OVER the note,
  reached by one button that exists only where the rail cannot be. Split does not
  degrade into Focus: the pile stays one tap away, which is the whole
  proposition, and the button is the only thing the phone adds.

  ⚠ THE BUDGET IS MEASURED, because plan 45 proved this is the number that
  decides a capture surface — its fields-in-the-box cost 319px of chrome at
  390px and left *~16px of words* on a 375×667 with the keyboard up. Drive the
  bar's `phone keyboard` control and read it here: at 390×780 with `--kb: 300px`
  the drawer is 480px, chrome is 177px, and **303px of words survive**. That is
  the number to argue with.

  ⚠ ONE SOURCE, FOUR RENDERS — the /lab/chrome rule. There is one pile, one
  editor and one drawer; a proposition is `data-v` on the wrapper plus where the
  shell is put. So no variant can be a prettier copy of another, and the whole
  experiment deletes in one file.

  ⚠ THE SAMPLE DECIDES THE WINNER, so the pile is mixed on purpose. Four of
  these are jottings, and under `focus` a jotting costs a full-screen drawer to
  fix a typo — which is the case that can lose it the decision. The monster at
  the bottom is the case that raised the question. Judge both, and remember the
  pile you actually keep is mostly the short ones.
*/
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import EditorToolbar from '../../components/admin/EditorToolbar.astro';
import LinkDialog from '../../components/admin/LinkDialog.astro';
import { renderMarkdown } from '../../lib/markdown';
// The Observatory's primitives, exactly as an admin page gets them: `hq.css`
// from AdminLayout, `admin.css` inlined by `app.css` (which `Base` brings).
import '../../styles/hq.css';

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

/**
 * The four propositions. `today` is a CONTROL, not a candidate — without it the
 * other three are judged against a memory of the problem rather than against
 * the problem.
 *
 * `cost` is what shipping it would take, stated so the cheap answer cannot win
 * by looking expensive or vice versa.
 */
const VARIANTS = [
  {
    key: 'today',
    label: 'Today',
    claim: 'In the card, unbounded. What ships right now.',
    cost: 'nothing — this is the shipped room',
  },
  {
    key: 'capped',
    label: 'Capped',
    claim: 'In the card, but the surface stops at 55vh and scrolls inside itself.',
    cost: '~8 lines of CSS, no script',
  },
  {
    key: 'focus',
    label: 'Focus',
    claim: 'The same editor, lifted into a near-fullscreen drawer. Nothing to fill in.',
    cost: 'a drawer shell + ~40 lines: one more destination for a shell that already travels',
  },
  {
    key: 'split',
    label: 'Split',
    claim: 'The drawer, with the rest of the pile as a rail you can move between.',
    cost: 'Focus, plus a rail and note-to-note handover without closing',
  },
] as const;

/**
 * The pile. Written as Markdown because a dump genuinely is Markdown since
 * 2026-08-06, and the monster carries headings, a list and bold so the drawer
 * is judged against a document with structure rather than against a wall.
 */
const DUMPS = [
  {
    id: 'a',
    ago: '11 minutes ago',
    body: 'Ask the dentist about the night guard when they call back — and whether the old one can be re-fitted rather than remade.',
  },
  {
    id: 'b',
    ago: '2 hours ago',
    body: 'A room that shows the words is a different promise from a room that shows the titles.\n\nKeep that sentence.',
  },
  {
    id: 'c',
    ago: 'yesterday',
    body: `The thing I keep circling: every tool I have ever liked was one that let me put something down without first deciding what it was. The ones I abandoned all asked a question at the door — which notebook, which project, which tag — and the question is never expensive on its own. It is expensive because it arrives at the exact moment the thought is least willing to sit still.

So the test for anything I build here is not how good it is once you are inside it. It is what it costs to get in.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'd',
    ago: 'two days ago',
    body: `### What the check-in is actually for

Not a mood tracker. A mood tracker asks you to rate a day, and a rating is a summary — by the time you can give one honestly the day has already been flattened into a number you will not recognise in a month.

The check-in asks smaller questions and keeps the answers as words, which means:

- the answer is re-readable later, and a number is not
- near-synonyms are allowed to both stand, because the gap between them is the information
- an unanswered day stays unanswered rather than defaulting to fine

**The failure mode to watch** is the form growing a field every time I notice something the form did not capture. That is how a check-in becomes a chore, and a chore gets skipped, and a skipped day is worth less than a shallow one.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'e',
    ago: 'four days ago',
    body: `## Reading dump — attention, and why the observatory metaphor keeps holding

Long one, sorry. Getting it down before it evaporates.

### The claim I want to steal

The book's argument is that attention is not a resource you spend but a *shape* you hold. You do not run out of it the way you run out of money; you lose the shape and then everything you look at afterwards is looked at with the wrong instrument. That is a much better account of what a bad day feels like than "I was tired." I was not tired. I was holding the wrong shape and every input got processed by it.

If that is right then the useful question at the end of a day is not *how much did I get done* but *what shape was I in, and what put me there*. Which is more or less what the check-in already asks, by accident. Worth making deliberate.

### Where it argues with itself

Two chapters later there is a long section on interruption that quietly reverts to the resource model — interruptions are described as costing you something, with a recovery time, which is exactly the accounting the first half rejected. I do not think this is a contradiction so much as an admission that the resource model is easier to write policy with. You can say "protect two hours" and be understood. "Protect the shape" is true and unactionable.

That gap is interesting. Most of the useful frameworks I have ever adopted are the *less* true one that could be acted on, plus a private awareness of the truer one that could not.

### The bit about instruments

There is a lovely passage about observatories — that the point of a telescope is not that it makes things bigger but that it makes a *specific* kind of seeing repeatable. Anyone can point at the sky and be impressed. The instrument is what lets you come back next week and know whether what you saw has moved.

That is the whole argument for writing things down, stated better than I have managed. A jotting is not a record of a thought. It is the second observation that makes the first one mean something, and you cannot make it without an instrument you trust enough to use badly.

**Which is the actual test for this room**: can I use it badly. Not can I use it carefully.

### Things to look up

- the source for the "shape not resource" framing — the endnote points at a paper I have not read and I suspect the book is doing a lot of work the paper does not support
- whether the interruption-cost numbers are the old open-office studies, which were bad
- who first used the observatory analogy; it feels borrowed and uncredited

### The part I disagree with outright

The prescription at the end is a routine, and it is a routine of the worst kind: it assumes the reader's problem is that they have not yet decided to be different. Every routine that has ever worked for me worked because it lowered the cost of a thing I already wanted to do. None of them worked because I resolved harder. The book knows this — it says so in the middle — and then ends with a resolution anyway, because that is what the last chapter of this kind of book is contractually required to be.

### What I actually take from it

1. The end-of-day question should be about shape, not output.
2. An instrument earns its place by being usable carelessly.
3. Coming back to a thing is the observation; the first look is only a claim.
4. Be suspicious of any framework whose final chapter is a resolution.

Enough. Most of this is one idea in four costumes, and the one idea is the observatory one.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'f',
    ago: 'last week',
    body: 'Song for the constellation about leaving — the one with the false ending. Check whether the live version is the one with the extra verse.',
  },
] as const;

/**
 * Rendered and measured exactly as the room does it — `breaks: true` because a
 * dump's newlines are its shape, and the same `long` formula so the clamp and
 * the `more` control appear on the same cards they would appear on for real.
 */
/**
 * THE FOUR WAYS OUT, PLUS DELETE — the pile's own chooser, transplanted
 * verbatim from `notes.astro` rather than reinvented. The order is FREQUENCY,
 * not symmetry: a dump is far more often an errand than an essay seed, and a
 * line somebody said is commoner than a piece in waiting.
 *
 * ⚠ THEY ARE THE SAME FIVE IN BOTH SPELLINGS BELOW. What the bench asks is
 * where they live in a drawer, never which ones exist — inventing a sixth here
 * would be testing a room this building does not have.
 */
const WAYS_OUT = [
  {
    key: 'agenda',
    icon: 'ph:calendar-blank',
    label: 'Add to the Agenda…',
    echo: 'opens the real TaskSheet, prefilled and model-parsed',
  },
  { key: 'log', icon: 'ph:note-pencil', label: 'Log an entry…', echo: 'opens the LogSheet, which asks who first' },
  {
    key: 'quote',
    icon: 'ph:quotes',
    label: 'Make it a quote…',
    echo: 'creates a quote fragment — a different KIND, and it wants attribution',
  },
  {
    key: 'piece',
    icon: 'ph:pen-nib',
    label: 'Make a piece',
    echo: 'one status flip: note → draft, same id, same text, same history',
  },
  {
    key: 'append',
    icon: 'ph:article',
    label: 'Add to a piece…',
    echo: 'the one genuine copy — the dump is consumed, and there is no undo',
  },
] as const;

const notes = DUMPS.map((d) => ({
  ...d,
  html: renderMarkdown(d.body, { breaks: true }),
  long: d.body.split('\n').length > 10 || d.body.length > 700,
}));
---

<Base title="Notes — the editor bench">
  <div class="lab" data-v="today" data-acts="spread">
    <header class="lab__head">
      <h1>A full editor for the pile — four propositions</h1>
      <p>
        The pile clamps a long dump to 16rem when reading and lets it grow without limit when editing. Everything below
        is about that one asymmetry. Press <b>✎</b> on the monster near the bottom under each proposition; the numbers on
        the right are measured while you do it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>⚠ Narrow the window before concluding anything.</b> At 1280px the monster below opens a ~1300px editor; the same
        note at a phone's 390px is roughly four times that, because the measure is a third as wide. Every number here reads
        at its most flattering on a desktop.
      </p>
      <p class="lab__real">
        <b>Real:</b> the editor, the toolbar, one instance moved between cards, the pile's CSS, the numbers. <b>Not:</b>
        the save (identical under all four — nothing here writes to the database), images, and the → / 🗑 actions, which are
        drawn so the card is the right shape and do nothing.
      </p>
    </header>

    {
      /* The control bar. Sticky, because half of what is being judged is what
        the PAGE does while you edit — a bar that scrolled away would take the
        readout with it exactly when the readout matters. */
    }
    <div class="lab__bar">
      <div class="lab__variants">
        {
          VARIANTS.map((v) => (
            <button type="button" class="lab__v" data-v-pick={v.key} aria-pressed={v.key === 'today'}>
              <span class="lab__v-label">{v.label}</span>
              <span class="lab__v-claim">{v.claim}</span>
              <span class="lab__v-cost">{v.cost}</span>
            </button>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      {
        /* MEASURED, NOT CLAIMED. `reachable` is the one with a judgement inside
          it, so the judgement is written down: a card behind an open modal is
          NOT reachable — the drawer traps focus and light-dismisses — so Focus
          scores 0 by definition while its backdrop is up, and Split scores its
          rail. Under Today and Capped it is the honest count of other cards
          intersecting the viewport. */
      }
      {
        /* THE SECOND QUESTION, ADDED 2026-08-26 AFTER THE FIRST LOOK. Michael,
          on Split: *"I feel like we're wasting some opportunity in that view to
          include more actions like make this a quote, append to writing,
          delete… it's missing a lot of obvious ui ux."*

          He is right that the drawer was empty chrome, and the fix is not
          simply "put the card's foot in it" — because the card's foot is the
          way it is for a reason that a drawer does not share. 14 · Piece 2
          settled THREE controls, not six: *"six grey glyphs under a card that
          might hold three words is the 'many elements competing for attention'
          smell, and the four destinations are not four questions anyway: they
          are one question, asked once."*

          Every clause of that argument is about a CARD. A full-height drawer
          has room, is already a deliberate place you chose to be, and holds one
          document rather than a pile of them — so the answer may honestly
          differ. Both spellings are built; neither is the default. */
      }
      <div class="lab__acts-pick">
        <span class="lab__acts-label">actions in the drawer</span>
        <button type="button" data-acts-pick="tucked" aria-pressed="false">behind →</button>
        <button type="button" data-acts-pick="spread" aria-pressed="true">spread out</button>
      </div>

      {
        /* ⚠ THE PHONE KEYBOARD, FAKED HONESTLY — and it is here because plan 45
          proved this is the measurement that decides a capture surface: chrome
          cost 319px at 390px and left *~16px of words* on a 375×667 with the
          keyboard up. A drawer full of chrome can fail the same way and pass
          every desktop look.

          `--kb` is the real variable (`scripts/keyboard-inset.ts`), and
          `.drawer-dialog` already sizes itself with it — `height: calc(100dvh -
          var(--kb))`. So setting it by hand drives the SHIPPED rule rather than
          simulating one. 300px is a mid-range Android keyboard; the point is the
          order of magnitude, not the exact device. */
      }
      <div class="lab__acts-pick">
        <span class="lab__acts-label">phone keyboard</span>
        <button type="button" data-kb="0" aria-pressed="true">at rest</button>
        <button type="button" data-kb="300" aria-pressed="false">up (300px)</button>
      </div>

      <dl class="lab__meter">
        <div><dt>page height</dt><dd id="m-page">—</dd></div>
        <div><dt>editor visible</dt><dd id="m-editor">—</dd></div>
        <div><dt>other notes reachable</dt><dd id="m-reach">—</dd></div>
        <div><dt>taps this run</dt><dd id="m-taps">0</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <ul class="lab__pile" id="lab-pile">
      {
        notes.map((n) => (
          <li class="dump" data-note={n.id}>
            <div
              class:list={['dump__text', 'reading', 'jot-prose', n.long && 'dump__text--clamped']}
              data-text
              set:html={n.html}
            />
            {n.long && (
              <button type="button" class="dump__more" data-more>
                more
              </button>
            )}
            <textarea hidden data-edit-box set:text={n.body} />
            <div class="dump__foot">
              <time class="dump__stamp">{n.ago}</time>
              {/* ⚠ THE ⤢ IS ON EVERY CARD UNDER EVERY PROPOSITION, and that is
                  a deliberate simplification of the bench rather than a
                  proposal. The TRIGGER question — explicit ⤢, or does ✎ lift a
                  card the room already knows is long? — is downstream of the
                  surface question, and putting both on one bench would let a
                  good trigger rescue a bad surface. Under Today and Capped it
                  is inert and says so. */}
              <button
                type="button"
                class="dump__act"
                data-expand
                title="Open in the full editor"
                aria-label="Open in the full editor"
              >
                ⤢
              </button>
              <button type="button" class="dump__act" data-edit title="Edit" aria-label="Edit">
                ✎
              </button>
              <button
                type="button"
                class="dump__act"
                data-inert
                title="Turn it into… (not wired in the bench)"
                aria-label="Turn it into"
              >
                →
              </button>
              <button
                type="button"
                class="dump__act dump__act--danger"
                data-inert
                title="Delete (not wired in the bench)"
                aria-label="Delete"
              >
                🗑
              </button>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    {
      /* THE ONE EDITOR, and its home is outside the pile — the shipped room's
        arrangement, kept because it is the arrangement that makes Focus cheap.
        This element is what travels: into a card, or into the drawer. */
    }
    <div id="lab-shell" class="dump__shell" hidden>
      <div id="lab-editor"></div>
      <EditorToolbar class="dump__tools" />
    </div>

    {
      /* The drawer serves Focus AND Split — the rail is the only difference, and
        it is `hidden` under Focus. Two dialogs would be two shells to keep in
        step, which is the drift the one-source rule exists to prevent.

        ⚠ `.drawer-dialog` IS ADMIN.CSS'S, NOT A COPY. It brings the right-edge
        slide, the backdrop, the exit animation and `height: calc(100dvh -
        var(--kb))` — so the phone keyboard is already handled here, which is
        one of the costs Focus does NOT have to pay. */
    }
    <dialog id="lab-drawer" class="drawer-dialog w-full" aria-label="Full editor">
      <div class="lab__drawer" id="lab-drawer-shell">
        {
          /* ⚠ THE RAIL IS A COLUMN OF ITS OWN, FLOOR TO CEILING, and the foot
            stops where it stops. Michael, 2026-08-26: *"lets make it so that the
            footer only extends to the point where the sidebar is… I want it to
            be more unambiguous or intuitive that those actions belong to the
            specific note in view."*

            That is the whole layout argument and it is a correct one. A foot
            spanning both columns is making a claim about the drawer; these five
            act on ONE note, and a control bar that underlines the list it does
            not act on is the kind of ambiguity you feel without naming. So the
            drawer is now two panes side by side — the pile, and the note — and
            everything about the note lives inside the note's pane.

            ⚠ THE HEAD MOVED WITH IT, and that was not asked for. The same
            argument covers it exactly: the stamp and the word count are facts
            about the note in view, so leaving them in a strip across the top
            while the foot narrowed would have applied the principle to half the
            chrome and left the other half contradicting it. Only ✕ is about the
            drawer, and it stays with the note pane because that is where a hand
            already is. */
        }
        {
          /* The way back out of the rail on a phone — a scrim over the note,
            never over the rail itself. Above the breakpoint it is `display:
            none` and inert. */
        }
        <div class="lab__rail-scrim" data-rail-scrim></div>
        <aside class="lab__rail" id="lab-rail-pane" aria-label="The rest of the pile">
          <div class="lab__rail-head">
            <span class="lab__rail-title">The pile</span>
            {
              /* ⚠ BENCH CHROME, MARKED AS SUCH, AND IT EARNS ITS PLACE BY BEING
                UNREACHABLE OTHERWISE. The bar's copy of this toggle is behind a
                modal backdrop once the drawer is up, so comparing the two
                spellings meant closing and reopening — which reads as friction
                in Split rather than in the bench.

                ⚠ IT LIVES IN THE RAIL, NOT IN THE NOTE'S HEAD, by the same rule
                this whole layout now follows: it is about the bench, not about
                the note in view. */
            }
            <span class="lab__acts-pick lab__acts-pick--in">
              <span class="lab__acts-label">bench</span>
              <button type="button" data-acts-pick="tucked" aria-pressed="false">behind →</button>
              <button type="button" data-acts-pick="spread" aria-pressed="true">spread</button>
            </span>
          </div>
          {
            /* Rows are built from the cards at open time rather than rendered
              here, so the rail cannot describe a pile that is one edit out of
              date. */
          }
          <div class="lab__rail-rows" id="lab-rail"></div>
        </aside>

        <div class="lab__note">
          {
            /* ⚠ THE HEAD SAYS WHAT THIS IS AND WHEN IT MOVED, AND NOTHING ELSE.
              No title field, no slug, no tabs — that is the line plan 14 drew
              and the one thing a drawer for a jotting must not cross. The word
              count is here because a drawer is where a long dump is read, and it
              is the one fact about a 900-word note the card cannot show. */
          }
          <header class="lab__drawer-head">
            {
              /* The way to the pile when there is no room for it beside the
                words. Absent above the breakpoint, where the rail is simply
                there — a control that duplicates a visible one is worse than no
                control (10-hq §10b). */
            }
            <button type="button" class="lab__pile-btn" id="lab-pile-btn" aria-expanded="false">
              <Icon name="ph:list" /> The pile
            </button>
            <span class="lab__drawer-title" id="lab-drawer-title">Note</span>
            <span class="lab__drawer-stamp" id="lab-drawer-stamp">
              <Icon name="ph:clock-light" /><span data-ago></span>
            </span>
            <span class="lab__drawer-words" id="lab-drawer-words"></span>
            <button type="button" class="lab__drawer-x" data-drawer-close aria-label="Close">✕</button>
          </header>

          <div class="lab__doc" id="lab-drawer-slot"></div>

          {
            /* THE FOOT — the half that was missing. Both spellings live here and
              `data-acts` picks one, so they cannot drift into being two
              different sets of destinations dressed differently.

              ⚠ SPREAD IS THE DEFAULT SINCE 2026-08-26 — Michael, on seeing both:
              *"spread out is better than behind the arrow."* The toggle stays so
              the losing spelling can still be looked at rather than remembered.

              ⚠ DELETE IS OUTSIDE THE TOGGLE, in the corner, under both. It is
              not a way OUT of the pile — it is the end of the thought — and
              putting it in a row with five reversible destinations is how a menu
              teaches you to aim carefully instead of reading (hq.css,
              `.pop__row--danger`). */
          }
          <footer class="lab__foot">
            <div class="lab__foot-acts">
              <button type="button" class="lab__foot-btn" data-open-chooser>
                <Icon name="ph:arrow-right" />
                <span class="lab__foot-word">Turn it into…</span>
              </button>
              {
                WAYS_OUT.map((w) => (
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    class="lab__foot-btn lab__foot-btn--spread"
                    data-way={w.key}
                    data-echo={w.echo}
                    title={w.label}
                    aria-label={w.label}
                  >
                    <Icon name={w.icon} /> <span class="lab__foot-word">{w.label}</span>
                  </button>
                ))
              }
            </div>
            {
              /* Where an inert action reports itself — /lab/capture's rule: a
                bench prints the row it WOULD write rather than pretending. */
            }
            <span class="lab__echo" id="lab-echo" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
            <button
              type="button"
              class="lab__foot-btn lab__foot-btn--danger"
              data-way="delete"
              data-echo="soft-deletes the row and drops the undo strip — 8 seconds to change your mind"
              title="Delete"
              aria-label="Delete"
            >
              <Icon name="ph:trash" />
              <span class="lab__foot-word">Delete</span>
            </button>
          </footer>
        </div>

        {
          /* The chooser, for the `tucked` spelling — the pile's own popover markup.

            ⚠ INSIDE THE DIALOG, AND IT HAD TO MOVE HERE. The first cut put it at
            page level, where the shipped room keeps it. It rendered perfectly:
            open, anchored, `:popover-open` true, in the top layer, painted above
            the drawer — and completely dead. `showModal()` makes everything
            OUTSIDE the dialog inert, and a top-layer popover is no exception, so
            every row was unclickable while looking right; `elementFromPoint` at
            the menu's own centre returned the rail behind it. Trap 7 in a new
            costume — typecheck, build and a screenshot all pass.

            ⚠ AND THE COST THIS WAS EXPECTED TO CARRY IS NOT REAL, which is the
            other half worth writing down. `.drawer-dialog` sets `translate`, and
            a non-`none` translate normally makes an element a containing block
            for `position: fixed` descendants — so `.pop` + `anchorPopover`, which
            think in viewport coordinates, looked like they would land ~320px off
            once they moved in here. They do not: a TOP-LAYER element's containing
            block is the viewport whatever its ancestors do. Measured rather than
            reasoned — anchored at left 77px it renders at x=77 either side of the
            move. The shipped primitives survive the drawer unchanged. */
        }
        <div class="pop pop--wide" popover id="lab-chooser">
          {
            WAYS_OUT.map((w) => (
              <button type="button" class="pop__row" data-way={w.key} data-echo={w.echo}>
                <Icon name={w.icon} /> {w.label}
              </button>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </dialog>

    <LinkDialog id="lab-link-dialog" />
  </div>
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* ⚠ EVERYTHING IS SCOPED UNDER `.lab` BY HAND, and the block is `is:global`
     rather than scoped because TipTap builds its editable surface at runtime —
     a node created after the build carries no scope attribute, so a scoped rule
     could never reach the one element this bench is about. */
  .lab {
    max-width: 64rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem 6rem;
  }
  .lab__head h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .lab__head p {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
